var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#AstroRoutines","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"The AstroRoutines module provides low-level routines for classical astrodynamics. It currently  covers conversions between the anomalies of elliptic and hyperbolic orbits, the two-body quantities of a single state, and the circular restricted three-body problem in the rotating frame. For the three-body problem it provides the equations of motion, their Jacobian, the Jacobi constant, the five libration points, and the variational equations for the state transition matrix. The routines are primarily function-based rather than struct-based. The anomaly conversions follow Vallado (2013), and the three-body formulation follows Koon, Lo, Marsden, and Ross (2000). The three-body routines are verified against the NASA/JPL SSD three-body periodic orbit database.\n\nReferences:\n\nVallado, D. A. (2013), Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications, 4th ed., Microcosm Press / Springer.\nKoon, W. S., Lo, M. W., Marsden, J. E., and Ross, S. D. (2000), Heteroclinic connections between periodic orbits and resonance transitions in celestial mechanics, Chaos 10(2), 427–469.","title":"AstroRoutines"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"Convert between the three anomalies of an elliptic orbit. Angles are in radians.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\ne = 0.2                                   # eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1\nM = 1.0                                   # mean anomaly\n\nE = mean_to_eccentric_anomaly(M, e)       # solves Kepler's equation\nν = eccentric_to_true_anomaly(E, e)\n1.3793207953216657\n\nEvaluate the three-body dynamics at a state. The mass ratio is the only parameter, and the state is [x, y, z, vx, vy, vz] in the rotating frame.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = 1.215058560962404e-02                            # Earth–Moon\ns  = [0.81596252146384562, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.20722124749217649, 0.0]\n\nC  = jacobi_constant(s, mu)                           # the integral of motion\nds = zeros(6)\ncr3bp_eom!(ds, s, mu)                                 # derivative, for a solver\nC, ds[4]\n(3.150016832809122, 0.20116656604362226)\n\nLocate the libration points of that system.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = 1.215058560962404e-02\nlibration_point(mu, :L1), libration_point(mu, :L4)\n([0.8369151257723572, 0.0, 0.0], [0.48784941439037594, 0.8660254037844386, 0.0])","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Anomaly-conversions","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"AstroRoutines provides all six conversions among mean anomaly M, eccentric anomaly E, and true anomaly ν, so any pair converts directly rather than through a chain.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\ne = 0.2\nM = 1.0\n\nE = mean_to_eccentric_anomaly(M, e)\nν = eccentric_to_true_anomaly(E, e)\n\n# Every pair converts directly, so the round trip returns the angle you started with\ntrue_to_mean_anomaly(ν, e) - M\n0.0\n\nAngles are in radians. Outputs are single-revolution and unwrapped: M and E return as the smooth continuation of the input, and ν returns in the principal branch (−π, π]. The routines do not apply [0, 2π) normalization, which would introduce a point where the derivative does not exist.\n\nmean_to_eccentric_anomaly and mean_to_true_anomaly iterate, and accept tol (default 1e-12) and maxiter (default 50). The other four are closed form. Eccentricity outside [0, 1) throws ArgumentError.\n\nAll six are differentiable with ForwardDiff with respect to both arguments, including the two that iterate.\n\nusing AstroRoutines, ForwardDiff\n\nForwardDiff.derivative(m -> mean_to_true_anomaly(m, 0.2), 1.0)\n1.1456167281730383","title":"Anomaly conversions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Hyperbolic-orbits","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"On a hyperbola the hyperbolic anomaly H takes the place of E, and Kepler's equation becomes M = e·sinh H − H. Four conversions cover it: true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly, hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly, hyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly, and mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\ne = 1.5\nM = 2.0                                   # time from periapsis, in units of 1/n\n\nH = mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(M, e)      # solves the hyperbolic Kepler equation\nν = hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly(H, e)\n\nhyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly(true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(ν, e), e) - M\n4.440892098500626e-16\n\nM and H are negative before periapsis and positive after. Neither is an angle, and neither wraps. ν lies between the asymptotes, cos ν > −1/e; a true anomaly beyond them throws ArgumentError, as does an eccentricity of 1 or less. mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly iterates from a starter after Danby (1987), which keeps Newton's method well behaved close to a parabola, and takes the same tol and maxiter keywords as its elliptic counterpart. All four are differentiable with ForwardDiff.","title":"Hyperbolic orbits"},{"category":"section","location":"#Two-body-quantities","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"These are closed forms of one state: the position r and velocity v of a body relative to a central body of gravitational parameter μ, in consistent units.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nμ = 398600.4418                           # Earth, km³/s²\nr = [7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0]               # km\nv = [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9]                      # km/s\n\nspecific_energy(r, v, μ), orbital_period(r, v, μ), periapsis_speed(r, v, μ)\n(-28.209205204146848, 5909.984957722748, 7.596164151814829)\n\nFunction Value\nspecific_energy(r, v, μ) v²/2 − μ/r\nangular_momentum(r, v), angular_momentum_magnitude(r, v) r × v and its length\norbit_normal(r, v) unit vector along r × v\norbital_angular_velocity(r, v) (r × v)/r²\neccentricity_vector(r, v, μ) points to periapsis, length e\norbital_period(r, v, μ) 2π·√(a³/μ), closed orbits\nmean_motion(r, v, μ) √(μ/abs(a)³), closed orbits and hyperbolas\nhyperbolic_excess_speed(r, v, μ) √(2ε), escape orbits\napoapsis_speed(r, v, μ), periapsis_speed(r, v, μ) μ(1 ∓ e)/h; apoapsis on closed orbits\n\nA quantity the orbit does not have, such as the period of a hyperbola, throws ArgumentError naming the orbit's energy or eccentricity rather than returning NaN. Each is differentiable with ForwardDiff with respect to r, v, and μ.\n\nIn Epicycle the same functions also take a spacecraft, specific_energy(sat) or orbital_period(sat, EarthMJ2000Ec), and read its state in the frame given.","title":"Two-body quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-circular-restricted-three-body-problem","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"These routines use the rotating frame and normalized units: the primaries sit at (−μ, 0, 0) and (1−μ, 0, 0), their separation is 1, their orbital rate is 1, and the total mass is 1. The only parameter is the mass ratio μ, and every state argument is the six-element vector [x, y, z, vx, vy, vz].\n\ncr3bp_mass_ratio computes μ from the two primary masses, so the value carries whichever mass constants you supply. The Earth–Moon databases at JPL are built on 1.215058560962404e-02, which is the value used below.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = cr3bp_mass_ratio(5.9722e24, 7.342e22)\n0.012144329283018118\n\nlibration_point takes :L1 through :L5 and returns the three-vector [x, y, 0]. The collinear points are computed by Newton iteration rather than tabulated, since their positions move with μ; L4 and L5 are closed form. A mass ratio outside (0, 1), or a symbol other than the five, throws ArgumentError.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = 1.215058560962404e-02\nlibration_point(mu, :L1)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.8369151257723572\n 0.0\n 0.0\n\nFour functions evaluate the dynamics at a state. cr3bp_accel returns the acceleration, cr3bp_jacobian the 6×6 ∂f/∂s, and jacobi_constant the integral of motion. cr3bp_eom! writes the full derivative into a six-element vector, in the signature an ODE solver expects; it is ballistic motion only, so a propagator that applies thrust adds the control acceleration to ds[4:6] after calling it.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = 1.215058560962404e-02\n\n# An Earth–Moon L1 Lyapunov orbit from the JPL SSD database\ns = [0.81596252146384562, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.20722124749217649, 0.0]\n\na = cr3bp_accel(s, mu)\nA = cr3bp_jacobian(s, mu)\nC = jacobi_constant(s, mu)\n\nds = zeros(6)\ncr3bp_eom!(ds, s, mu)\nC, ds\n(3.150016832809122, [0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0, 0.20116656604362226, 0.0, -0.0])","title":"The circular restricted three-body problem"},{"category":"section","location":"#State-transition-matrix","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_stm_eom! evaluates the equations of motion and the variational terms together, writing the derivative of a 42-element vector: the six-element state, then the state transition matrix Φ flattened column-major. The variational block is Φ̇ = A(s) Φ, with A the analytic Jacobian cr3bp_jacobian returns. Integration is the caller's, so the same integrator advances the state and Φ on the same steps and to the same tolerance. cr3bp_stm_initial packs a six-element state into that layout with Φ(0) = I. Either function throws ArgumentError on a vector of the wrong length.\n\nusing AstroRoutines\n\nmu = 1.215058560962404e-02\ns0 = [0.81596252146384562, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.20722124749217649, 0.0]\n\nz0 = cr3bp_stm_initial(s0)\ndz = zeros(42)\ncr3bp_stm_eom!(dz, z0, mu)\nlength(z0)\n42","title":"State transition matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"The entry points of each area. The full list follows below.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.mean_to_eccentric_anomaly","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"mean_to_eccentric_anomaly(M::Real, e::Real; tol::Real=1e-12, maxiter::Integer=50)\n\nConvert mean anomaly to eccentric anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nSolves Kepler's equation M = E - e·sin(E) for E.\n\nArguments\n\nM: Mean anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\ntol: Convergence tolerance in radians on the Newton step |ΔE|; default 1e-12.\nmaxiter: Maximum Newton iterations (default 50).\n\nReturns\n\nThe eccentric anomaly E [rad], as the smooth continuation of M (not wrapped to [0, 2π)).\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to M and e (ForwardDiff): the analytic partials are dE/dM = 1 / (1 - e·cos(E)) and dE/de = sin(E) / (1 - e·cos(E)).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\nThrows ErrorException if the iteration does not converge within maxiter.\n\nExample\n\nmean_to_eccentric_anomaly(deg2rad(120.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n2.250008654463779\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.mean_to_eccentric_anomaly"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(M::Real, e::Real; tol::Real=1e-12, maxiter::Integer=50)\n\nConvert mean anomaly to hyperbolic anomaly for a hyperbolic orbit by solving the hyperbolic Kepler equation M = e·sinh H − H with Newton iteration.\n\nArguments\n\nM: Mean anomaly.\ne: Eccentricity, e > 1.\n\nKeywords\n\ntol: Convergence tolerance on the Newton step, relative to max(1, |H|).\nmaxiter: Maximum number of Newton iterations.\n\nReturns\n\nThe hyperbolic anomaly H, with the sign of M.\n\nNotes\n\nStarts from H₀ = sign(M)·ln(2|M|/e + 1.8), after Danby (1987), which stays near the root even close to a parabola, where the derivative e·cosh H − 1 vanishes at H = 0. Newton converges within 20 iterations for e from 1 + 10⁻⁶ to 10⁴ and |M| up to 10⁵.\nDifferentiable with respect to M and e (ForwardDiff); convergence is judged on the value, so the derivatives are those of the converged root.\nThrows ArgumentError if e ≤ 1, and an error if the iteration does not converge.\n\nExample\n\nmean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(1.0, 1.5)\n\n# output\n\n1.1616354445046073\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.specific_energy","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"specific_energy(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nSpecific orbital energy of a two-body state, ε = v²/2 − μ/r.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nThe energy per unit mass [km²/s²]: negative on a closed orbit, zero on a parabola, positive on a hyperbola.\n\nExample\n\nspecific_energy([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n-28.209205204146848\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.specific_energy"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_mass_ratio","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_mass_ratio(m1::Real, m2::Real)\n\nMass ratio of a circular restricted three-body system.\n\nArguments\n\nm1: Mass of the larger primary, in any unit.\nm2: Mass of the smaller primary, in the same unit.\n\nReturns\n\nμ = m2 / (m1 + m2), the dimensionless mass ratio the other routines here take. Gravitational parameters may be passed instead of masses; the ratio is the same.\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError unless both masses are positive.\n\nExample\n\ncr3bp_mass_ratio(3.986004418e5, 4.9028e3)   # Earth-Moon, from the GMs\n\n# output\n\n0.012150583916324809\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_mass_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.libration_point","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"libration_point(mu::Real, which::Symbol)\n\nPosition of one of the five libration points of a circular restricted three-body system.\n\nArguments\n\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1.\nwhich: :L1, :L2, :L3, :L4 or :L5.\n\nReturns\n\nThe three-vector [x, y, 0] in normalized rotating-frame units.\n\nNotes\n\nL1, L2 and L3 lie on the x-axis and are iterated to a residual below 1e-14, so they carry that much error rather than being exact. L4 and L5 are closed form, at x = 1/2 − μ and y = ±√3/2.\nThrows ArgumentError for μ ∉ (0, 1) or a symbol outside the five.\n\nExample\n\nlibration_point(0.01215058560962404, :L1)   # Earth-Moon L1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.libration_point"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_eom!","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_eom!(ds, s::AbstractVector, mu::Real)\n\nWrite the time derivative of a circular restricted three-body state into ds.\n\nArguments\n\nds: Six-element output, overwritten.\ns: State [x, y, z, ẋ, ẏ, ż] in normalized units.\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The derivative is written into ds.\n\nNotes\n\nBallistic motion only. A propagator that applies thrust adds the control acceleration to ds[4:6] after calling this.\n\nExample\n\nds = zeros(6)\ncr3bp_eom!(ds, [0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0],\n           0.01215058560962404)\nds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_eom!"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroRoutines.jacobi_constant","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"jacobi_constant(s::AbstractVector, mu::Real)\n\nJacobi constant of a state in the circular restricted three-body problem.\n\nArguments\n\ns: State [x, y, z, ẋ, ẏ, ż] in normalized units.\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nC = 2U − v², where U is the pseudo-potential.\n\nNotes\n\nConstant along a ballistic arc, and changes only when something does work on the spacecraft. Holding it along an integrated arc checks the integration tolerance; along a low-thrust arc its change measures the energy the thrust supplied.\nSign convention: C decreases as speed increases, and the forbidden region for a given C is where 2U < C.\nDifferentiable with respect to the state and μ (ForwardDiff).\n\nExample\n\njacobi_constant([0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0],\n                0.01215058560962404)\n\n# output\n\n3.150016832809122\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.jacobi_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.angular_momentum-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"angular_momentum(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector)\n\nSpecific angular momentum vector of a two-body state, h = r × v.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\n\nReturns\n\nThe 3-component vector h [km²/s], normal to the orbit plane.\n\nExample\n\nangular_momentum([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9])\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -8610.0\n -6780.0\n 51920.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.angular_momentum"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.angular_momentum_magnitude-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"angular_momentum_magnitude(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector)\n\nMagnitude of the specific angular momentum of a two-body state, ‖r × v‖.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\n\nReturns\n\n‖h‖ [km²/s].\n\nExample\n\nangular_momentum_magnitude([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9])\n\n# output\n\n53063.98873058828\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.angular_momentum_magnitude"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.apoapsis_speed-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"apoapsis_speed(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nSpeed at apoapsis of the closed two-body orbit through a state, vₐ = μ(1 − e)/h.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nvₐ [km/s].\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError unless the eccentricity is below 1.\n\nExample\n\napoapsis_speed([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n7.42722369879468\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.apoapsis_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_accel-Tuple{AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_accel(s::AbstractVector, mu::Real)\n\nAcceleration of the third body in the rotating frame of a circular restricted three-body system.\n\nArguments\n\ns: State [x, y, z, ẋ, ẏ, ż] in normalized units.\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1. See cr3bp_mass_ratio.\n\nReturns\n\nThe three-vector [ẍ, ÿ, z̈] in normalized units, carrying the Coriolis, centrifugal and gravitational terms and no thrust. A control acceleration is added by the caller.\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to the state and μ (ForwardDiff).\nSingular at the primaries themselves, where r1 or r2 is zero.\n\nExample\n\ncr3bp_accel([0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0],\n            0.01215058560962404)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_accel"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_jacobian-Tuple{AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_jacobian(s::AbstractVector, mu::Real)\n\nJacobian of the circular restricted three-body equations of motion with respect to the state.\n\nArguments\n\ns: State [x, y, z, ẋ, ẏ, ż] in normalized units.\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe 6x6 matrix ∂ṡ/∂s. The upper right block is the identity, the lower left is the Hessian of the pseudo-potential, and the lower right carries the Coriolis terms.\n\nNotes\n\nAnalytic, not a finite difference, so it carries no step-size error.\nDifferentiable with respect to the state and μ (ForwardDiff).\nSingular at the primaries themselves, where r1 or r2 is zero.\nThis is the A(s) of the variational equation Φ̇ = A(s) Φ that cr3bp_stm_eom! evaluates.\n\nExample\n\ncr3bp_jacobian([0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0],\n               0.01215058560962404)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_stm_eom!-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_stm_eom!(dz, z::AbstractVector, mu::Real)\n\nWrite the derivative of a state augmented with its state transition matrix.\n\nArguments\n\ndz: 42-element output, overwritten.\nz: [s; vec(Φ)] — the six-element state followed by the 6x6 state transition matrix in column-major order, 42 elements in all.\nmu: Mass ratio, 0 < μ < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The derivative is written into dz.\n\nNotes\n\nThe variational block is Φ̇ = A(s) Φ, where A is cr3bp_jacobian. This function evaluates that derivative; integration is the caller's, so the same integrator advances the state and Φ on the same steps and to the same tolerance.\nStart from cr3bp_stm_initial, which sets Φ(0) = I.\nThe flow is Hamiltonian, so Φ stays symplectic and det Φ = 1 along an exact arc. That determinant is the well-conditioned check on an integration tolerance; the symplectic residual scales with the square of the matrix norm, which is large on an unstable orbit.\nThrows ArgumentError unless z has 42 elements.\n\nExample\n\nz0 = cr3bp_stm_initial([0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0])\ndz = zeros(42)\ncr3bp_stm_eom!(dz, z0, 0.01215058560962404)\ndz[1:6]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_stm_eom!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.cr3bp_stm_initial-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"cr3bp_stm_initial(s::AbstractVector)\n\nBuild the augmented initial condition cr3bp_stm_eom! expects.\n\nArguments\n\ns: The six-element state the arc starts from.\n\nReturns\n\nA 42-element vector: the state followed by the identity matrix flattened column-major, which is the state transition matrix at the start of any arc.\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError unless s has six elements.\n\nExample\n\ncr3bp_stm_initial([0.8159625214638456, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2072212474921765, 0.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.cr3bp_stm_initial"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.eccentric_to_mean_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"eccentric_to_mean_anomaly(E::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert eccentric anomaly to mean anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nEvaluates Kepler's equation M = E - e·sin(E) (closed form).\n\nArguments\n\nE: Eccentric anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe mean anomaly M [rad], as the smooth continuation of E (not wrapped to [0, 2π)).\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to E and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\nThis is the exact analytic inverse of mean_to_eccentric_anomaly and serves as its truth source.\n\nExample\n\neccentric_to_mean_anomaly(deg2rad(125.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n2.017831156135114\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.eccentric_to_mean_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.eccentric_to_true_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"eccentric_to_true_anomaly(E::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert eccentric anomaly to true anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nEvaluates the half-angle relation ν = 2·atan(√(1+e)·sin(E/2), √(1−e)·cos(E/2)).\n\nArguments\n\nE: Eccentric anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe true anomaly ν [rad] in the principal branch (−π, π].\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to E and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\n\nExample\n\neccentric_to_true_anomaly(deg2rad(125.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n2.337781537541991\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.eccentric_to_true_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.eccentricity_vector-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"eccentricity_vector(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nEccentricity vector of a two-body state, e = ((v² − μ/r)·r − (r·v)·v) / μ.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nThe 3-component vector e, pointing to periapsis, with magnitude the eccentricity.\n\nExample\n\neccentricity_vector([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.005542985399239766\n -0.009541538750105955\n -0.0021651913908546376\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.eccentricity_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_excess_speed-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"hyperbolic_excess_speed(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nSpeed at infinity of the escape trajectory through a state, v∞ = √(2ε).\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nv∞ [km/s].\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError unless the specific energy is positive.\n\nExample\n\nhyperbolic_excess_speed([7000.0, 300.0, 0.0], [0.0, 12.5, 1.0], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n6.5930724754364824\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_excess_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"hyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly(H::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert hyperbolic anomaly to mean anomaly for a hyperbolic orbit.\n\nEvaluates the hyperbolic Kepler equation M = e·sinh H − H in closed form.\n\nArguments\n\nH: Hyperbolic anomaly.\ne: Eccentricity, e > 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe mean anomaly M, negative before periapsis and positive after. It is not an angle and does not wrap.\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to H and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ≤ 1.\nExact; the truth the iterative mean_to_hyperbolic_anomaly is checked against.\n\nExample\n\nhyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly(1.0, 1.5)\n\n# output\n\n0.7628017904657021\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_to_mean_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly(H::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert hyperbolic anomaly to true anomaly for a hyperbolic orbit.\n\nEvaluates the half-angle relation ν = 2·atan(√((e+1)/(e−1))·tanh(H/2)).\n\nArguments\n\nH: Hyperbolic anomaly.\ne: Eccentricity, e > 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe true anomaly ν [rad], in (−ν∞, ν∞) with cos ν∞ = −1/e; negative before periapsis.\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to H and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ≤ 1.\nExact analytic inverse of true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly.\n\nExample\n\nhyperbolic_to_true_anomaly(0.5283553629664819, 1.5)\n\n# output\n\n1.0471975511965976\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.mean_motion-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"mean_motion(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nMean motion of the two-body orbit through a state, n = √(μ/|a|³) with a = −μ/(2ε).\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nThe mean motion [rad/s]. On a hyperbola it is the rate of the hyperbolic mean anomaly.\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError on a parabola (zero specific energy).\n\nExample\n\nmean_motion([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n0.0010631474279759658\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.mean_motion"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.mean_to_true_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"mean_to_true_anomaly(M::Real, e::Real; tol::Real=1e-12, maxiter::Integer=50)\n\nConvert mean anomaly to true anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nSolves Kepler's equation for E, then converts E to ν.\n\nArguments\n\nM: Mean anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\ntol: Convergence tolerance passed to the Kepler solve (default 1e-12).\nmaxiter: Maximum Newton iterations for the Kepler solve (default 50).\n\nReturns\n\nThe true anomaly ν [rad] in the principal branch (−π, π].\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to M and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\nThrows ErrorException if the Kepler solve does not converge within maxiter.\n\nExample\n\nmean_to_true_anomaly(deg2rad(120.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n2.3975613842919206\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.mean_to_true_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.orbit_normal-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"orbit_normal(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector)\n\nUnit normal to the orbit plane of a two-body state, along r × v.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\n\nReturns\n\nThe 3-component unit vector ĥ.\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError when r × v = 0, a radial trajectory, which has no orbit plane.\n\nExample\n\norbit_normal([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9])\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.16225693179067105\n -0.12777026684561554\n  0.9784413354903183\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.orbit_normal"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.orbital_angular_velocity-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"orbital_angular_velocity(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector)\n\nAngular velocity vector of the position of a two-body state about the central body, ω = (r × v) / r².\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\n\nReturns\n\nThe 3-component vector ω [rad/s], along the orbit normal.\n\nExample\n\norbital_angular_velocity([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9])\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.00017039382545022759\n -0.00013417771620819315\n  0.0010275084108450426\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.orbital_angular_velocity"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.orbital_period-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"orbital_period(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nPeriod of the closed two-body orbit through a state, T = 2π·√(a³/μ) with a = −μ/(2ε).\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nThe period [s].\n\nNotes\n\nThrows ArgumentError unless the specific energy is negative.\n\nExample\n\norbital_period([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n5909.984957722748\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.orbital_period"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.periapsis_speed-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"periapsis_speed(r::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector, μ::Real)\n\nSpeed at periapsis of the two-body orbit through a state, vₚ = μ(1 + e)/h.\n\nArguments\n\nr: Position relative to the central body, 3 components [km].\nv: Velocity relative to the central body, 3 components [km/s].\nμ: Gravitational parameter of the central body [km³/s²].\n\nReturns\n\nvₚ [km/s], for any conic.\n\nExample\n\nperiapsis_speed([7000.0, 300.0, 1200.0], [-0.4, 7.4, 0.9], 398600.4418)\n\n# output\n\n7.596164151814829\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.periapsis_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.true_to_eccentric_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"true_to_eccentric_anomaly(ν::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert true anomaly to eccentric anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nEvaluates the half-angle relation E = 2·atan(√(1−e)·sin(ν/2), √(1+e)·cos(ν/2)).\n\nArguments\n\nν: True anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe eccentric anomaly E [rad] in the principal branch (−π, π].\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to ν and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\nExact analytic inverse of eccentric_to_true_anomaly on a single revolution.\n\nExample\n\ntrue_to_eccentric_anomaly(deg2rad(130.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n2.1037839463996946\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.true_to_eccentric_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(ν::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert true anomaly to hyperbolic anomaly for a hyperbolic orbit.\n\nEvaluates H = asinh(√(e²−1)·sin ν / (1 + e·cos ν)), the form of tanh(H/2) = √((e−1)/(e+1))·tan(ν/2) that stays finite across ν = ±π.\n\nArguments\n\nν: True anomaly [rad], between the asymptotes, 1 + e·cos ν > 0. A value and the same value less 2π give the same H.\ne: Eccentricity, e > 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe hyperbolic anomaly H, negative before periapsis and positive after. It is not an angle and does not wrap.\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to ν and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ≤ 1, or if ν lies outside the asymptotes.\nExact analytic inverse of hyperbolic_to_true_anomaly.\n\nExample\n\ntrue_to_hyperbolic_anomaly(deg2rad(60.0), 1.5)\n\n# output\n\n0.5283553629664819\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.true_to_hyperbolic_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroRoutines.true_to_mean_anomaly-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"AstroRoutines","text":"true_to_mean_anomaly(ν::Real, e::Real)\n\nConvert true anomaly to mean anomaly for an elliptic orbit.\n\nConverts ν to E, then evaluates Kepler's equation M = E - e·sin(E).\n\nArguments\n\nν: True anomaly [rad].\ne: Eccentricity, 0 ≤ e < 1.\n\nReturns\n\nThe mean anomaly M [rad], as the smooth continuation of ν (not wrapped to [0, 2π)).\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable with respect to ν and e (ForwardDiff).\nThrows ArgumentError if e ∉ [0, 1).\nClosed form, so there is no tolerance to set and the cost does not depend on e.\n\nExample\n\ntrue_to_mean_anomaly(deg2rad(130.0), 0.2)\n\n# output\n\n1.9315253702414643\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroRoutines.true_to_mean_anomaly"}]
}
